    {
      "id": "504ff8e4-0712-4989-af52-52ef340ee9f6",
      "domain": "Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design",
      "difficulty": "Easy",
      "question": "In a Software as a Service (SaaS) model, which security layer is the PRIMARY responsibility of the cloud customer?",
      "options": [
        "Physical security of the data center",
        "Operating system patching and hardening",
        "Data governance and access management",
        "Hypervisor and network configuration"
      ],
      "correct_answer": 2,
      "explanation": "**Correct Answer:** C. Data governance and access management\n\n**Explanation (CISSP Manager Logic):**\nIn the SaaS model, the provider manages the entire technology stack including the infrastructure, OS, and application. The customer remains responsible for the data they put into the system and who is granted access to it.\n\n**By focusing on data governance, you:**\n- Ensure compliance with data residency and privacy laws\n- Prevent unauthorized internal access via Least Privilege\n- Maintain accountability for organizational information assets\n\n**While the other options** are relevant, they fall short because:\n- **A:** Physical security is always the sole responsibility of the cloud provider.\n- **B:** Operating system management is handled by the provider in SaaS and PaaS models.\n- **D:** Infrastructure and virtualization layers are managed by the provider in SaaS.\n\n**Think like a manager:**\nRegardless of the service model, the customer can never outsource the ultimate responsibility for their own data and its lifecycle.",
      "tags": [
        "shared responsibility model",
        "saas",
        "data security"
      ],
      "created_by": null,
      "is_active": true,
      "created_at": "2026-02-10T04:57:01.967+00:00",
      "updated_at": "2026-02-10T04:57:01.967+00:00",
