    {
      "id": "daf40c58-3204-40fd-bafc-6cb0df83bb1f",
      "domain": "Cloud Data Security",
      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "question": "An organization implementing Bring Your Own Key (BYOK) wants to ensure the provider cannot access the plaintext key. Which mechanism provides the BEST assurance of key protection?",
      "options": [
        "Generating the key within a local hardware security module",
        "Uploading the key via a Transport Layer Security (TLS) tunnel",
        "Using the cloud provider's native key generation service",
        "Encrypting the key with the provider's master root key"
      ],
      "correct_answer": 0,
      "explanation": "**Correct Answer:** A. Generating the key within a local hardware security module\n\n**Explanation (CISSP Manager Logic):**\nFrom a risk management perspective, the root of trust must remain with the data owner. By generating the key in a local, physical HSM before exporting it to the cloud, the organization ensures it maintains exclusive control over the key generation process and entropy.\n\n**By implementing this solution, you:**\n- Establish a verifiable root of trust outside the cloud environment\n- Prevent the cloud provider from ever seeing the key generation process\n- Maintain compliance with strict data sovereignty and residency requirements\n\n**While the other options** are relevant, they fall short because:\n- **B:** TLS only protects the key during transit, not the underlying security of the key itself.\n- **C:** Native generation gives the cloud provider control over the key lifecycle, defeating the purpose of BYOK.\n- **D:** Encrypting with the provider's root key grants the provider the ability to decrypt and access the key material.\n\n**Think like a manager:**\nControl over the key lifecycle is the only way to ensure data remains confidential in a multi-tenant environment where the underlying infrastructure is not owned by the organization.",
      "tags": [
        "encryption and key management",
        "byok",
        "hsm"
      ],
      "created_by": null,
      "is_active": true,
      "created_at": "2026-02-10T04:57:04.143+00:00",
      "updated_at": "2026-02-10T04:57:04.143+00:00",
